PRACTICE — lesson 10: answer questions about a log with awk
-----------------------------------------------------------
/work/app.log is a web shop's request log. Each line is:
  time  user  status  message (any number of words)  ms
The last word is always the request's duration in milliseconds.
Don't change /work/app.log.

1. Write every request slower than 1000 ms to
   /work/report/slow.txt as "user ms", one per line, in log
   order, like: tom 2300
2. Write the total time per user to /work/report/users.txt as
   "user total", one per line, biggest total first.
   Careful: the messages don't all have the same number of words.

Check: sh /practice/check.sh     Answer: /practice/solution.md
[exit 0]

# check PRZED rozwiązaniem (oczekiwane FAIL)
$ sh /practice/check.sh
PASS  app.log is unchanged
FAIL  /work/report/slow.txt does not exist yet
FAIL  /work/report/users.txt does not exist yet

RESULT: FAIL (2 check(s) failed)
[exit 1]

# solution.md
$ cd /work && awk '$NF > 1000 {print $2, $NF}' app.log > report/slow.txt

[exit 0]

# check po CZĘŚCI rozwiązania (oczekiwane FAIL)
$ sh /practice/check.sh
PASS  app.log is unchanged
PASS  slow.txt lists the 4 slow requests as "user ms"
FAIL  /work/report/users.txt does not exist yet

RESULT: FAIL (1 check(s) failed)
[exit 1]

# solution.md
$ cd /work && awk '{t[$2] += $NF} END {for (u in t) print u, t[u]}' app.log | sort -k2 -rn > report/users.txt

[exit 0]

# check PO solution.md (oczekiwane PASS)
$ sh /practice/check.sh
PASS  app.log is unchanged
PASS  slow.txt lists the 4 slow requests as "user ms"
PASS  users.txt has the total per user, biggest first

RESULT: PASS — well done
[exit 0]

